// std/hardware.dag — the single authority for the hardware-manufacturer entity.
//
// DESIGN.md §3 single-authority: the manufacturer entity "AMD" (or Intel, Apple,
// Qualcomm) is ONE thing whether it ships a CPU, a GPU, or an accelerator. Before
// this module that entity forked into two enums — std.cpu.types.CpuVendor and
// product.compute_fabric.GpuVendor — that each re-listed the shared makers
// (Amd/Intel/Apple/Qualcomm) under a different authority. HardwareVendor folds both
// into one (net concepts shrink 2 -> 1), and the still-stringly AcceleratorFacts.vendor
// grounds onto it as well.
//
// NOT std.types.Vendor: that "Vendor" is the target-triple vendor field (Pc/Apple/W64/
// UnknownVendor) — an unrelated concept that only collides on the name. Kept separate
// on purpose; renaming that one is a distinct std-conflation follow-up.
//
// Component-validity is a SEPARATE fact, not a reason to fork the entity. Not every
// HardwareVendor makes every component (Nvidia ships GPUs, not CPUs in this set;
// Ampere/Arm ship CPUs, not GPUs). That constraint is enforced fail-closed at the
// point of use (e.g. gpu_compute_capability_sm_label refuses non-GPU makers) rather
// than by a second value-set enum.
module std.hardware

type HardwareVendor = Intel | Amd | Nvidia | Ampere | Arm | Apple | Qualcomm
